70947e-06,-6.437280717364047e-06,-2.1457441107486375e-05,-8.177422569133341e-05,-0.5816006064414978,-4.768370445162873e-07,0.0,-0.0016124354442581534,-0.8009342551231384,-1.3157315254211426,0.0,0.0,-0.012214032001793385,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-0.00015567521040793508,0.0,-0.003542815800756216,-0.5277774930000305,-7.152555099310121e-07,-0.012016408145427704,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-7.92710343375802e-05,0.0,0.0,-6.437280717364047e-06,-2.47952248173533e-05,-3.2186455882765586e-06,-1.1920922133867862e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,2629,315,279,9904,311,198,78045,17,1124,15940,61,18,856,481,220,18,1124,15940,856,284,481,59,37018,90,18,15170,17,92,1124,15940,220,17,87,59,60,258,279,9873,400,15,1124,273,856,1124,273,220,17,1124,2493,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of the solutions to\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\]in the interval $0 \\le x \\le 2 \\pi.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"d50fb52595b342df7bd590fe9bef5cf82357be1e6cb2f0131665e44e20b85987"},"seconds":21.806904554367065,"seed":377910625805037523,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the equation \\(2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\), we start by using the double-angle identity for sine, \\(\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x\\). Substituting this into the equation, we get:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we have:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nNext, we can factor out \\(\\sin x\\) from both sides of the equation:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3) = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nWe can rearrange this to:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x) = 0.\\]\nThis equation gives us two cases to consider:\n1. \\(\\sin x = 0\\),\n2. \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\).\n\n**Case 1: \\(\\sin x = 0\\).**\nThe solutions to \\(\\sin x = 0\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi\\).\n\n**Case 2: \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\).**\nWe use the Pythagorean identity \\(\\sin^2 x = 1 - \\cos^2 x\\) to substitute for \\(\\sin^2 x\\):\n\\[2 (1 - \\cos^2 x) - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0.\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[2 - 2 \\cos^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0,\\]\n\\[-2 \\cos^2 x + 3 \\cos x - 1 = 0.\\]\nMultiplying through by \\(-1\\) gives:\n\\[2 \\cos^2 x - 3 \\cos x + 1 = 0.\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(\\cos x\\). We can solve it using the quadratic formula \\(y = \\cos x\\):\n\\[2y^2 - 3y + 1 = 0.\\]\nThe solutions to this quadratic equation are:\n\\[y = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 8}}{4} = \\frac{3 \\pm 1}{4}.\\]\nSo, the solutions are:\n\\[y = 1 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = \\frac{1}{2}.\\]\nThus, \\(\\cos x = 1\\) or \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nFor \\(\\cos x = 1\\), the solution in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) is \\(x = 0, 2\\pi\\). However, these solutions are already included in the solutions from Case 1.\n\nFor \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\), the solutions in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nCombining all the solutions from both cases, we have \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nThe sum of these solutions is:\n\\[0 + \\pi + 2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = 0 + \\pi + 2\\pi + 2\\pi = 4\\pi.\\]\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is:\n\\[\\boxed{4\\pi}.\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{4\\pi}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:64"}
